\begin_layout Subsection
Construction of leave-one-out auxiliary estimates
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "sec:construction-LOO-general"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
As elucidated in the rank-1 matrix denoising example in Section
\begin_inset space ~
\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "sec:rank-1-denoising-LOO"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

, the key to enabling fine-grained analysis is to seek assistance from a
 collection of leave-one-out estimates.
 Akin to Section
\begin_inset space ~
\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "sec:LOO-estimates-denoising"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

, we construct for each 
\begin_inset Formula $1\leq l\leq n$
\end_inset

 two auxiliary matrices 
\begin_inset Formula $\bm{M}^{(l)}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\bm{E}^{(l)}=\big[E_{i,j}^{(l)}\big]_{1\leq i,j\leq n}$
\end_inset

 as follows 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align}
\bm{M}^{(l)}\coloneqq\bm{M}^{\star}+\bm{E}^{(l)},\quad E_{i,j}^{(l)}\coloneqq\begin{cases}
E_{i,j}, & \text{if }i\neq l\text{ and }j\neq l,\\
0, & \text{else},
\end{cases}\label{eq:Ml-construction-general}
\end{align}

\end_inset

which are generated by simply discarding all random noise appearing in the
 
\begin_inset Formula $l$
\end_inset

-th column/row of the data matrix.
 In addition, let 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda_{1}^{(l)},\cdots,\lambda_{n}^{(l)}$
\end_inset

 be the eigenvalues of 
\begin_inset Formula $\bm{M}^{(l)}$
\end_inset

 sorted by 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align}
\big|\lambda_{1}^{(l)}\big|\geq\big|\lambda_{2}^{(l)}\big|\geq\cdots\geq\big|\lambda_{n}^{(l)}\big|,
\end{align}

\end_inset

and denote by 
\begin_inset Formula $\bm{u}_{i}^{(l)}$
\end_inset

 the eigenvector of 
\begin_inset Formula $\bm{M}^{(l)}$
\end_inset

 associated with 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda_{i}^{(l)}$
\end_inset

.
 The leave-one-out spectral estimates 
\begin_inset Formula $\bm{U}^{(l)}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\bm{\Lambda}^{(l)}$
\end_inset

 are, therefore, given by 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align}
\bm{U}^{(l)}\coloneqq\big[\bm{u}_{1}^{(l)},\cdots,\bm{u}_{r}^{(l)}\big]\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times r};\quad\bm{\Lambda}^{(l)}\coloneqq\mathsf{diag}\big(\big[\lambda_{1}^{(l)},\cdots,\lambda_{r}^{(l)}\big]\big).
\end{align}

\end_inset

We emphasize again that the main advantage of introducing the leave-one-out
 estimate 
\begin_inset Formula $\bm{U}^{(l)}$
\end_inset

 stems from its statistical independence from the 
\begin_inset Formula $l$
\end_inset

-th row of 
\begin_inset Formula $\bm{M}$
\end_inset

, which substantially simplifies the analysis for the 
\begin_inset Formula $l$
\end_inset

-th row of the estimate.
 In principle, our analysis employs the leave-one-out estimates to help
 decouple delicate statistical dependency in a row-by-row fashion.
 Another crucial aspect of the analysis lies in the exploitation of the
 proximity of all these auxiliary estimates, a feature that is enabled by
 certain 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

stability
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 of the spectral method from the Davis-Kahan theorem.
\end_layout

